    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The (re)contamination of surface waters and sediment from stormwater runoff is a major concern at Department of Defense (DoD) facilities. Decentralized, rapid- flow geomedia biofilters may be an attractive solution for treating stormwater runoff at these sites. Commonly found contaminants at DoD facilities targeted in this study include: polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs), polycyclic aromatic hydro- carbons (PAHs), per- and polyfluoroalkyl compounds (PFASs), heavy metals, and a suite of hydrophilic organic compounds. Biofilters with black carbon, such as biochar and regenerated activated carbon, and zeolite/carbonate sand amendments will be tested at the lab-scale for contaminant removal at various infiltration rates. Rapid-flow geomedia biofilters have numerous applications apart from use at DoD facilities, and may play an important role more broadly in urban stormwater control strategies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Pritchard, J. C., Spahr, S., Cho, Y. M., Fitzgerald, N. J. M., Higgins, C. P., & Luthy, R. G. (2019). N3.08 Pritchard 2019 ReNUWIt Annual Meeting Poster. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/cg586ct8955",
            "type": "preferred citation",
